#{[17592186115875 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\cos (\\frac{1}{2}x)$."] [17592186115876 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  (π/8)^{+}}\\tan (4x)$."] [17592186115877 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  (π/4)^{+}}\\tan (x + \\frac{π}{4})$."] [17592186115882 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$” is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it is a removable discontinuity).\n\n- Statement “It is **not** continuous at $x=1$ but it is differentiable at $x=1$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement “It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-2,2)$” is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement “It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-2,2)$” is true."] [17592186116413 :tutorial/content "A **rooted forest** is a graph in which each connected component is a rooted tree.\n\nThe number of labeled rooted forests on $n$ vertices is\n\n$$\n\\mid F_n \\mid = (n+1)^{n-1}\n$$\n\nThis result follows from the bijection between the set $F_n$ of all labeled rooted forests on $n$ vertices and the set $T_{n+1}$ of all labeled trees on $n+1$ vertices. Since these sets are in one-to-one correspondence, they have the same cardinality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{F}_{n}| & =|{T}_{n+1}| \\\\  & =(n+1)^{(n+1)-2} \\\\  & =(n+1)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, the number of labeled rooted forests on $3$ vertices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid F_{3} \\mid &= (3 + 1)^{3 - 1} \\\\\n&= 4^{2} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186116432 :tutorial/content "For any constant function\n\n$$\nf(x)=c\n$$\n\nthe limit $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a} c$ will match the value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a} c=c \\,\n$$\n\nFor example, the limit $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2} 1$ is just the value of the constant, $1$:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2} 1 = 1\n$$\n\nWe can verify this result by graphing the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1\n$$\n\n![](images/a2/a2c17e59dca9161db435fc4b3edf78a22fed7e6ee94219ac29b85627116791ac.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we compute the left and right-sided limits:\n\n- Left-sided limit: $$\n\\displaystyle \\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} 1 = 1\n$$\n- Right-sided limit: $$\n\\displaystyle \\hspace{.25cm} \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} 1 = 1\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits both evaluate to $1$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2} 1 = 1 \\,\n$$"] [17592186116433 :tutorial/content "The **constant rule for limits** states that if $c$ is a constant, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}c\\cdot f(x) = c\\cdot \\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=c\\cdot L\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2}5x^3,\n$$ we just factor the constant $5$ out of the limit and then substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2}{\\color{blue}5}x^3 &= {\\color{blue}5} \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 2}x^3 \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{blue}5}\\cdot 2^3 \\\\[5pt] &= 40 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, consider the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ below. Suppose we know that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,1} cf(x) = 10}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a real constant. Let's find the value of $c$.\n\n![](images/de/dea6bb963e83928f463ac2b6d09775b0c11ac0ca7c8e3e567c4b51da154c86f9.png)\n\nFrom the figure above, we get that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\, 1} f(x)} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  1}cf(x) &= 10 \\\\\nc \\cdot lim_(x → 1)f(x) &= 10 \\\\\nc \\cdot 5 &= 10 \\\\\nc &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186116437 :tutorial/content "To find the limit of a vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually. So, if $\\mathbf f (t) = x(t) \\ \\mathbf {i} + y(t) \\ \\mathbf {j} +z(t) \\ \\mathbf {k}$ is our vector function, then the limit is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to a} \\mathbf f (t) = \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a} x(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {i} + \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}y(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {j} +\\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}z(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {k}\n$$\n\nThe limit on the left exists *if and only if* all the limits on the right exist too.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left( 5+t \\right) \\ \\mathbf {i} +  (t^{2}-1) \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\sin{t} \\ \\mathbf {k},\n$$\n\nand we want to compute the limit $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf f (t).\n$$ To do this, we find the limit of each component function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\,\\underbrace{\\lim_{t\\to 0}(5+t)}_{=\\,5}\\,]i+[\\,\\underbrace{\\lim_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-1)\\,}_{=\\,-1}]j+[\\,\\underbrace{\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,}_{=\\,0}]k \\\\  & =5\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186116438 :tutorial/content "Many properties of limits of scalar functions extend naturally to limits of vector functions. For instance, if $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector functions and $p,q$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t \\to a}\\left[p \\ \\mathbf f(t) + q \\ \\mathbf g(t)\\right] =  p \\ \\lim_{t \\to a}\\mathbf f(t) + q \\ \\lim_{t \\to a}\\mathbf g(t).\n$$\n\nWe also have the following property for the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to a}‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to a}f(t)‖,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\| \\mathbf f(t) \\right\\|$ denotes the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf f(t)$."] [17592186116439 :tutorial/content "For two vector functions, the limit of a product is equal to the product of the limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to a}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to a}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to a}g(t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to a}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to a}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to a}g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $\\mathbf f(t)\\cdot \\mathbf g(t)$ and $\\mathbf f(t) \\times \\mathbf g(t)$ are the dot product and the cross product of $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t),$ respectively."] [17592186116443 :tutorial/content "Determining the continuity of a vector function is very similar to determining the continuity of a scalar function.  A vector function $\\mathbf f (t)$ is **continuous** at a point $t_0$ (in its domain) if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\left \\langle e^t,  \\dfrac{1}{t} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nis continuous at $t=1$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 1}({e}^{t}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{1},\\,\\frac{1}{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit of a vector function is determined by the limits of the components, a vector function is continuous at a point $t_0$ *if and only if* each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nWe say that a vector function is **continuous on the interval** $t\\in[a,b]$ if it is continuous at every point of this interval."] [17592186116470 :tutorial/content "Below are the graphs of the two most important trigonometric functions, $\\sin(x)$ and $\\cos(x).$\n\n![](images/29/29be90e6dd4312a97eb2fce107d7bf4d0158e98cb3e52b8df60548807fa2881c.png)\n\nFor any *finite* real number $a$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}\\sin x=\\sin (a) \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to a}\\cos x=\\cos (a).\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to 2\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the limits at infinity do not exist. As $x$ approaches $\\infty$ or $-\\infty$, the values of $\\sin(x)$ and $\\cos(x)$ oscillate between $1$ and $−1$ forever. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty} \\sin x=\\text{DNE}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty} \\cos x=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186116471 :tutorial/content "Below is the graph of the tangent function $y=\\tan(x).$\n\n![](images/a1/a1137c40e534ef7536e5f64d1e6d58e7440eb92e8dd8ff99954cb7c9ff8fab2f.png)\n\nNote that $\\tan x$ is not defined at the vertical asymptotes $x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi$ where $n$ is an integer. However, everywhere else, the limit of the tangent function is the value of the tangent function:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\tan (x)=\\tan\\left(a\\right),\\qquad a\\neq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi,\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x) &=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the limits at infinity do not exist. As $x$ approaches $\\infty$ or $-\\infty,$ the values of $\\tan(x)$ keep oscillating, and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty} \\tan x=\\text{DNE} .\n$$"] [17592186116472 :tutorial/content "Let's take another look at the graph of the tangent function $y=\\tan(x).$\n\n![](images/a1/a1137c40e534ef7536e5f64d1e6d58e7440eb92e8dd8ff99954cb7c9ff8fab2f.png)\n\nAs we can see from the graph, $$\n\\displaystyle x = \\pm \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pm \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, \\ldots\n$$ are the points where $y=\\tan (x)$ has vertical asymptotes. Both the left and right-sided limits at those points are infinite. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi/2^{-}}\\tan(x)=\\infty,\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to \\pi/2^{+}}\\tan(x)=-\\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi/2}\\tan(x)=\\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nIn general, at any vertical asymptote $x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi$ where $n$ is an integer, the limit is undefined:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi/2+n\\pi}\\tan(x)=\\textrm{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186116507 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **differentiable** at a point $x=a$ if the derivative $f'(a)$ exists.\n\nIn general:\n\n- If a function is not continuous at some point, then it is not differentiable at that point either.\n\n- If a function is differentiable at some point, then it must be continuous at that point.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given a step function $y=f(x)$ that has a jump discontinuity at $x=3,$ given below. We will verify that $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3$ by showing that $f'(3)$ does not exist. \n\n![](images/9f/9f91b034a27ce7fd2b432f0b094e4b40098247916b78f9c1b3eb64c7214f69b4.png)\n\nThe value $f'(3)$ is a limit, so to check whether it exists, we need to compute the left and right-sided limits.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) &=\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, we have that $f(x) = 2.$ So the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2-2}{x-3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, we have that $f(x)=1.$ So the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1-2}{x-3}=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{-1}{x-3}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the left and right-sided limits are not equal, the overall limit does not exist. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) &=\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}=\\text{DNE}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3.$"] [17592186118449 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}f(x) = 0"] [17592186118450 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}f(x) = DNE"] [17592186118451 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}f(x) =-1"] [17592186118452 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}f(x) = - ∞"] [17592186118453 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}f(x) = ∞"] [17592186118455 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}f(x) = 3"] [17592186118456 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}f(x) = DNE"] [17592186118457 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}f(x) = - ∞"] [17592186118458 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}f(x) = ∞"] [17592186118459 :answer/value "\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}f(x) = 1"] [17592186119528 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ What is $\\lim_{x \\to \\,-\\,4} f(x)?$\n\n![](images/ab/ab2abe13314c2005907dcd5d7077af8e87b7126474bbb0261f757d8ec7c00698.png)"] [17592186119528 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ grows to infinity, and therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -4^+} f(x)=\\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function $f(x)$ is not defined for $x<-4,$ and consequently we cannot approach $x=-4$ from the left. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -4^-} f(x)=\\text{DNE},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -4} f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186119535 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\,4} f(x).\n$$\n\n![](images/16/168433761f6629ad555a70acf5764f93246484c253f3c7ecba8fdfdfd87c2adf.png)"] [17592186119535 :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $4$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-\\infty.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 4^-} f(x) = -\\infty \\, .\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $4$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $-\\infty.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 4^+} f(x) = -\\infty \\, .\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 4} f(x)=-\\infty \\, .\n$$"] [17592186119677 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -3}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x+3}.\n$$"] [17592186119677 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -3}\\dfrac{x^2-9}{x+3} = \\dfrac{(-3)^2-9}{(-3)+3} = \\dfrac{0}{0} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} - 9}{x + 3} &= \\frac{x^{2} - 3^{2}}{x + 3} \\\\\n&= ((x + 3)(x - 3))/(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  - 3}\\frac{x^{2} - 9}{x + 3} &= \\lim_{x \\to  - 3}(x - 3) \\\\\n&=-3 - 3 \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119680 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 1}\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2+x}{x-1}.\n$$"] [17592186119680 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 1}\\dfrac{x^3-2x^2+x}{x-1} = \\dfrac{1^3-2(1)^2+1}{1-1} = \\dfrac{0}{0} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{3} - 2x^{2} + x}{x - 1} &= (x(x^{2} - 2x + 1))/(x - 1) \\\\\n&= (x(x - 1)^{2})/(x - 1) \\\\\n&= x(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  1}\\frac{x^{3} - 2x^{2} + x}{x - 1} &= \\lim_{x \\to  1}x(x - 1) \\\\\n&= 1(1 - 1) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119681 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 6}\\dfrac{x^2-6x}{x^2-x-30}.\n$$"] [17592186119681 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 6}\\dfrac{x^2-6x}{x^2-x-30} = \\dfrac{6^2-6\\cdot 6}{6^2-6-30} = \\dfrac{0}{0} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} - 6x}{x^{2} - x - 30} &= (x(x - 6))/((x - 6)(x + 5)) = \\frac{x}{x + 5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  6}\\frac{x^{2} - 6x}{x^{2} - x - 30} &= \\lim_{x \\to  6}\\frac{x}{x + 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{6 + 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120139 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  4}\\frac{x^{2} - x - 12}{x^{2} - 16}$."] [17592186120142 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  - 2}\\frac{2x + 4}{x^{2} - 3x - 10}$."] [17592186120236 :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-3,5,4⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨4,7,-1⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $B(-2,23,14)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $b=⟨-2,23,14⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =b+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨-2,23,14⟩+s⟨-3,5,4⟩+t⟨4,7,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120389 :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e03d079f04cc8d49991b109d5a5f853d4dc8d1309b17a773f9e2256d46e23dc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  - 1}f(x)$."] [17592186120390 :question/problem "![](images/96/966b85ec0f63e4991294bcf56ae0316a7415fa37b1338e8cfc06eaa7ae65c448.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}f(x)$."] [17592186120392 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b607f7d8606599ee31000ab847dde152762e7f9f0bbb4f43c05cdbac535c8f8.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  3^{+}}f(x) = - ∞$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  3^{-}}f(x) = ∞$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  3}f(x) = 3$"] [17592186120393 :question/problem "![](images/22/22ea4d16d4bf2e1e793e95c6d9fbc2f463123ad048d7f9ab8e88405e9c75096e.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-4)^{+}}f(x) = - ∞$\n2. $\\lim_{x \\to  (-4)^{-}}f(x) = ∞$\n3. $\\lim_{x \\to  - 4}f(x) = DNE$"] [17592186120405 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{y \\to  2}\\frac{3y^{2} - 12y + 12}{y^{2} - 4}$."] [17592186120406 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{y \\to  0}\\frac{y^{3} + 2y^{2}}{y^{2} + 3y}$."] [17592186120407 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{y \\to  - 3}\\frac{y^{3} + 6y^{2} + 9y}{2y^{2} - 2y - 24}$."] [17592186120467 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  1} - \\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x - 1}$."] [17592186120993 :tutorial/content "Until now, all limits of the form $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ that we have encountered have evaluated to either a finite number or $\\text{DNE}.$ However, there is another possibility: the limit could be *infinite*!\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. This function has a vertical asymptote at $x=1,$ and the function value $f(x)$ grows without bound as $x$ approaches the asymptote $x=1.$ What is the limit $\\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x)$? \n\n![](images/ab/ab9d57d3952fe0221616f9e64e1295fd0511703bdd5ed3a5fe91113cc7c8bf1b.png)\n\nRemember that in order for computing any limit, in this case, $\\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x),$ we have to compute the left and right-sided limits separately and ensure that they match up.\n\n- **Left-sided limit:** As we approach $x=1$ (the vertical asymptote) from the left side of the graph, the function $f(x)$ increases without bound, approaching $\\infty.$ We conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\infty.\n$$\n\n- **Right-sided limit:** Likewise, as we approach the vertical asymptote $x=1$ from the right side of the graph, $f(x)$ still approaches $\\infty.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^+ } f(x) = \\infty.\n$$\n\nWe see that both the left and right limits exist and are equal to $\\infty\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to 1^+} f(x) = \\infty\n$$\n\nAs a result, the overall limit is also equal to $\\infty\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1} f(x) = \\infty\n$$"] [17592186121006 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x}\n$$\n\nIf we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting $x=0$, we get a confusing result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  0}\\frac{x^{3} - 2x}{x^{2} + 4x} &= ((0)^{3} - 2(0))/((0)^{2} + 4(0)) = \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 0 0\n$$\n\nis called an **indeterminate form** because we are unable to determine the value of the limit from this expression.\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can factor the numerator and denominator and then cancel out any common factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} & = \\dfrac{x(x^2-2)}{x(x+4)} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{\\cancel{x}(x^2-2)}{\\cancel{x}(x+4)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the limit of the simplified fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^3-2x}{x^2+4x} &= \\lim_{x \\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{x^2-2}{x+4} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{(0)^2-2}{(0)+4} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{-2}{4} \\\\ & = -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186121434 :answer/value "\\lim_{h \\to  0}((3x^{2}y - 3x^{2}(y + h))/(h))"] [17592186121435 :answer/value "\\lim_{h \\to  0}((3(x + h)^{2}y + 3x^{2}y)/(h))"] [17592186121436 :answer/value "\\lim_{h \\to  0}((3x^{2}(y + h) - 3x^{2}y)/(h))"] [17592186121437 :answer/value "\\lim_{h \\to  0}((3x^{2}(y + h) + 3x^{2}y)/(h))"] [17592186121438 :answer/value "\\lim_{h \\to  0}((3(x + h)^{2}y - 3x^{2}y)/(h))"] [17592186121440 :answer/value "\\lim_{h \\to  0}\\left(\\frac{x\\sqrt{y + h} + x\\sqrt{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186121441 :answer/value "\\lim_{h \\to  0}(((x + h)\\sqrt{y} - x\\sqrt{y + h})/(h))"] [17592186121442 :answer/value "\\lim_{h \\to  0}(((x + h)\\sqrt{y} - x\\sqrt{y})/(h))"] [17592186121443 :answer/value "\\lim_{h \\to  0}\\left(\\frac{x\\sqrt{y + h} - x\\sqrt{y}}{h}\\right)"] [17592186121444 :answer/value "\\lim_{h \\to  0}((x\\sqrt{y + h} - (x + h)\\sqrt{y})/(h))"] [17592186122103 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x) = ∞$ and $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}f(x) = DNE$"] [17592186122104 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x) = ∞$ and $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}f(x) = 0$"] [17592186122105 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x) = 0$ and $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}f(x) = DNE$"] [17592186122106 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x) = ∞$ and $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}f(x) = - ∞$"] [17592186122107 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x) = 0$ and $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}f(x) = 0$"] [17592186124190 :question/worked-solution "The range of $-\\mid x \\mid$ is\n\n$$\n-\\mid x \\mid \\le 0\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts do not affect the range. So, we have\n\n$$\n-\\mid x+2 \\mid \\le 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n-3 \\mid x+2 \\mid \\le 0\n$$\n\nFinally, subtracting $1$ from the above inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3|x+2|-1 & \\le -1 \\\\ f(x) & \\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in (-\\infty,-1]$.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e666622c49e790190842a587c87019367866bc60c9284760029b43d09002b6c.png)"] [17592186124193 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x).\n$$ \n\n![](images/c7/c7ee917a93ed51c189979516b9ab0d3232c183921ee277f522f617518ce03fdf.png)"] [17592186124193 :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty$, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=2.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $2$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow -\\infty } f(x) = 2.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty$ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty } f(x) = \\infty.\n$$"] [17592186124234 :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\mid x \\mid$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mid x \\mid \\ge 0\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts do not affect the range. So,\n\n$$\n\\mid x-2 \\mid \\ge 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot & x - 2 & \\ge 3 \\cdot 0 \\\\ 3 & x - 2 & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, by adding $4$ to the above inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x-2| & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3|x-2|+4 & \\ge 4 \\\\ f(x) & \\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x) \\in [4,\\infty)$.\n\nWe can also see this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/77b2122ce7bdcf1caf974ef523f617c95e3026240719caac6dc4adfc5027ab90.png)"] [17592186124238 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$. Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} \\Bigl(3f(x) - g(x)\\Bigr).\n$$\n\n![](images/43/439ebf5a1e5812c6fb27da2dd4b3015ea757133343b86695ebad44db6eaeae7c.png)"] [17592186124238 :question/worked-solution "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} \\Bigl(3f(x) - g(x)\\Bigr) = 3 \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} f(x) - \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} g(x)\n$$\n\nThe above assumes that $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} g(x)$ both exist.\n\nIn the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} f(x) =-1\n$$\n\nHowever,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} g(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nbecause the left and right limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow {-2}^{-}}g(x)=3, \\qquad \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -2^{+}}g(x)=2 \\,\n$$\n\nBecause $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} f(x)$ exists while $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} g(x)$ does not, the overall limit $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} \\Bigl(3f(x) - g(x)\\Bigr)$ does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} \\Bigl(3f(x) - g(x)\\Bigr) = \\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186124256 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2)}\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{x+y} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot {2}^{3}}{(-1)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{1} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124257 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 0 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186124261 :question/worked-solution "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2(-x))}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186124271 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find the limit $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).\n$$ \n\n![](images/49/4982eb6e9020e5f6235049fd36a030a69d7b74a0e3ea60323ac8887f4c9bca79.png)"] [17592186124271 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $\\infty,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches $4,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\infty } f(x) = 4.\n$$"] [17592186124272 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\,1} \\left(6x^2+x\\right).\n$$"] [17592186124272 :question/worked-solution "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  1}(6x^{2} + x) &= \\lim_{x \\to  1}6x^{2} + \\lim_{x \\to  1}x \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot lim_(x → 1)x^{2} + \\lim_{x \\to  1}x \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 1^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= 6 + 1 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124273 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_ {x\\to (\\pi/4)} 2\\sec \\left(2x\\right).\n$$"] [17592186124273 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  (π/4)}2\\sec (2x) &= 2\\sec (2 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)) \\\\\n&= 2\\sec \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= (2)/(\\cos \\left(\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = 2\\sec\\left(2x\\right)\n$$\n\n![](images/24/2456c4bd5a25316fff60b7cc4de8a5562ac8f3a6144b1d45c042c26319eb7901.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\pi {4}\n$$\n\nfrom the left, the values of $y=2\\sec(2x)$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to (\\pi/4)^-} 2\\sec(2x) = \\infty\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\pi {4}\n$$\n\nfrom the right, the values of\n\n$$\ny=2\\sec (2x)\n$$\n\nare negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to (\\pi/4)^+} 2\\sec (2x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to (\\pi/4)} 2\\sec (2x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186124274 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_ {x \\to \\pi} \\csc x\n$$"] [17592186124274 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π}\\csc x &= \\csc π \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sin π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\csc\\left(x\\right)\n$$\n\n![](images/e4/e41e3d05507a407a9e8b84ecce4f5fa03315f8bb3a21ed1bf2255090fba11a91.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\pi$ from the left, the values of $y=\\csc x$ are positive and grow without bound.\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to \\pi^-}\\csc x = \\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $\\pi$ from the right, the values of $y=\\csc x$ are negative and decrease without bound.Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to \\pi^+}\\csc x = -\\infty\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to \\pi}\\csc x = \\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186124275 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_ {x \\to \\pi/3} \\cot \\left(3x\\right).\n$$"] [17592186124275 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/3}\\cot (3x) &= \\cot (3 \\cdot \\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\cot (π) \\\\\n&= (\\cos (π))/(\\sin (π)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\cot\\left(3x\\right)\n$$\n\n![](images/57/5711d4f447378c9e60c204b7e17d0f941ec87853507ce055f671df7af18371b0.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\pi 3\n$$\n\nfrom the left, the values of $y=\\cot(3x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to (\\pi/3)^-} \\cot(3x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\pi 3\n$$\n\nfrom the right, the values of $y=\\cot(3x)$ are positive and increase without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to (\\pi/3)^+} \\cot(3x) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to \\pi/3} \\cot \\left(3x\\right) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186124286 :question/problem "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} \\, .\n$$"] [17592186124286 :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/24/2497877ff3bc4a10871103f04502252843e2bdf8a9460c9ae4b32ff5344e921a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = -\\infty, \\qquad \\lim_{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = \\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}= \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186124289 :question/problem "Calculate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_ {x\\rightarrow -1} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} .\n$$"] [17592186124289 :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^4}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99f04a25115f084e40e9f8f9b06a6749532a2ba2320ee23d31af050a87957269.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty, \\qquad \\lim_{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}= \\infty.\n$$"] [17592186124367 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{2^2-4 \\cdot 1}{2-2\\sqrt{1}} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{(x-2\\sqrt{y})(x+2\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-(2\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-4y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y}) & =2+2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$"] [17592186124386 :question/problem "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{4x^4}.\n$$"] [17592186124386 :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $\\dfrac 1 4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^4} = \\dfrac 1 4\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$"] [17592186124387 :question/problem "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} \\, .\n$$"] [17592186124387 :question/worked-solution "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $(1-x)^4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} = 0.\n$$"] [17592186124392 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}.\n$$"] [17592186124392 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^4.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{4}+9x}{6{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{9x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124394 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}.\n$$"] [17592186124394 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^2.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{4-5x-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+3} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-2x-\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{-2x+0+0}{1+0}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124395 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).\n$$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{3}-15x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{15}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$"] [17592186124408 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).\n$$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}-10}{{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{8}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{1-0}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186124834 :question/problem "![](images/90/90f51ea4561fc1c1b892863daedc15bb813fbf18a03e1c9366d54f1a883d0db4.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $y = f(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  π/2}(2x + πf(x))$."] [17592186124864 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  1}(3x^{5} - 2x^{2} - x)$."] [17592186124872 :question/problem "![](images/c7/c78536f69ae8e8fe8ef866be3d4c64e278441861a3cc1eead349ed3d687f9ebf.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x)$. Find $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}f(x)$."]}
